    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> wrote it for the Communist League, which commissioned it after its Second Congress of 1847; it appeared anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in late February 1848.</li><li>It describes history through modes of production and class struggle, argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> will bring about its own destruction, and predicts a revolution leading to a classless society.</li><li>After the defeat of the 1848 revolutions it fell into obscurity, then revived from the early 1870s and spread widely after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Only one page of a draft of the Manifesto has ever been located. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-hobsbawm">Eric Hobsbawm</a> takes the absence of any other preliminary drafts as evidence of haste, after the Communist League sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> an ultimatum demanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> by 1 February 1848.</p></aside>
    </section>

